::ALG2_U05_L04_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L04_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.4; MLA.A2.FN.5; MLA.A2.FN.6<br><br>Question:<br>Describe g(x)=f(x)+8. {
= Shift up 8#Correct. Adding outside increases outputs by 8.
~ Shift down 8#That is f(x)-8.
~ Shift left 8#Inside change is needed.
~ Shift right 8#Inside change is needed.
}

::ALG2_U05_L04_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L04_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.4; MLA.A2.FN.5; MLA.A2.FN.6<br><br>Question:<br>Describe g(x)=f(x)-5. {
~ Shift up 5#That is f(x)+5.
= Shift down 5#Correct. Subtracting outside decreases outputs by 5.
~ Shift left 5#Inside change is needed.
~ Shift right 5#Inside change is needed.
}

::ALG2_U05_L04_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L04_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.4; MLA.A2.FN.5; MLA.A2.FN.6<br><br>Question:<br>Describe g(x)=f(x-7). {
~ Shift left 7#Inside direction is opposite.
~ Shift up 7#Outside change is needed.
= Shift right 7#Correct. Subtracting inside shifts right.
~ Shift down 7#Outside change is needed.
}

::ALG2_U05_L04_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L04_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.4; MLA.A2.FN.5; MLA.A2.FN.6<br><br>Question:<br>Describe g(x)=f(x+4). {
~ Shift right 4#Inside direction is opposite.
~ Shift up 4#Outside change is needed.
~ Shift down 4#Outside change is needed.
= Shift left 4#Correct. Adding inside shifts left.
}

::ALG2_U05_L04_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L04_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.4; MLA.A2.FN.5; MLA.A2.FN.6<br><br>Question:<br>Describe g(x)=-f(x). {
= Reflect across the x-axis#Correct. The outputs change sign.
~ Reflect across the y-axis#That would use f(-x).
~ Shift down 1#This is not a translation.
~ Vertical stretch by -1 only#The key graph effect is reflection across the x-axis.
}

::ALG2_U05_L04_Q06::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L04_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.4; MLA.A2.FN.5; MLA.A2.FN.6<br><br>Question:<br>Describe g(x)=f(-x). {
~ Reflect across the x-axis#That would be -f(x).
= Reflect across the y-axis#Correct. The inputs change sign.
~ Shift left 1#This is not a translation.
~ Shift right 1#This is not a translation.
}

::ALG2_U05_L04_Q07::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L04_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.4; MLA.A2.FN.5; MLA.A2.FN.6<br><br>Question:<br>Describe g(x)=2f(x). {
~ Horizontal stretch by factor 2#Inside multiplication affects horizontal scale.
~ Shift up 2#Adding outside shifts.
= Vertical stretch by factor 2#Correct. Multiplying outputs by 2 stretches vertically.
~ Reflect across y-axis#A negative input would reflect across y-axis.
}

::ALG2_U05_L04_Q08::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L04_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.4; MLA.A2.FN.5; MLA.A2.FN.6<br><br>Question:<br>Describe g(x)=0.5f(x). {
~ Vertical stretch by factor 2#This is the reciprocal effect.
~ Shift down 0.5#Multiplication is not translation.
~ Horizontal shift right 0.5#Inside subtraction is needed.
= Vertical compression by factor 0.5#Correct. Outputs are multiplied by one-half.
}

::ALG2_U05_L04_Q09::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L04_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.4; MLA.A2.FN.5; MLA.A2.FN.6<br><br>Question:<br>Which rule shifts f down 9? {
= g(x)=f(x)-9#Correct. Subtracting outside shifts down.
~ g(x)=f(x+9)#This shifts left.
~ g(x)=f(x-9)#This shifts right.
~ g(x)=f(x)+9#This shifts up.
}

::ALG2_U05_L04_Q10::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L04_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.4; MLA.A2.FN.5; MLA.A2.FN.6<br><br>Question:<br>Which rule shifts f right 3? {
~ g(x)=f(x+3)#This shifts left.
= g(x)=f(x-3)#Correct. Subtracting inside shifts right.
~ g(x)=f(x)+3#This shifts up.
~ g(x)=f(x)-3#This shifts down.
}

::ALG2_U05_L04_Q11::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L04_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.4; MLA.A2.FN.5; MLA.A2.FN.6<br><br>Question:<br>Which rule reflects f across the x-axis? {
~ g(x)=f(-x)#This reflects across y-axis.
~ g(x)=f(x)-1#This shifts down.
= g(x)=-f(x)#Correct. A negative outside changes output signs.
~ g(x)=f(x+1)#This shifts left.
}

::ALG2_U05_L04_Q12::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L04_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.4; MLA.A2.FN.5; MLA.A2.FN.6<br><br>Question:<br>Which rule reflects f across the y-axis? {
~ g(x)=-f(x)#This reflects across x-axis.
~ g(x)=f(x)+1#This shifts up.
~ g(x)=f(x-1)#This shifts right.
= g(x)=f(-x)#Correct. A negative inside changes input signs.
}

::ALG2_U05_L04_Q13::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L04_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.4; MLA.A2.FN.5; MLA.A2.FN.6<br><br>Question:<br>Describe g(x)=f(x-2)+6. {
= Shift right 2 and up 6#Correct. Inside -2 shifts right; outside +6 shifts up.
~ Shift left 2 and up 6#Inside direction is opposite.
~ Shift right 6 and up 2#The values are switched.
~ Shift left 2 and down 6#Both directions are wrong.
}

::ALG2_U05_L04_Q14::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L04_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.4; MLA.A2.FN.5; MLA.A2.FN.6<br><br>Question:<br>Describe g(x)=f(x+5)-1. {
~ Shift right 5 and down 1#Inside direction is opposite.
= Shift left 5 and down 1#Correct. Inside +5 shifts left; outside -1 shifts down.
~ Shift left 1 and down 5#The values are switched.
~ Shift right 1 and up 5#Directions and values are wrong.
}

::ALG2_U05_L04_Q15::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L04_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.4; MLA.A2.FN.5; MLA.A2.FN.6<br><br>Question:<br>Describe g(x)=-f(x)+4. {
~ Reflect across y-axis, then shift up 4#A negative input would reflect across y-axis.
~ Shift down 4 only#The reflection is missing.
= Reflect across x-axis, then shift up 4#Correct. The negative outside reflects outputs, then +4 moves up.
~ Shift right 4 only#No horizontal shift is shown.
}

::ALG2_U05_L04_Q16::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L04_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.4; MLA.A2.FN.5; MLA.A2.FN.6<br><br>Question:<br>Describe g(x)=3f(x-1). {
~ Shift left 1 and vertical stretch by 3#Inside direction is opposite.
~ Shift right 3 and up 1#This misreads the transformations.
~ Reflect across x-axis#No negative factor is present.
= Shift right 1 and vertical stretch by 3#Correct. Inside -1 shifts right; outside factor 3 stretches vertically.
}

::ALG2_U05_L04_Q17::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L04_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.4; MLA.A2.FN.5; MLA.A2.FN.6<br><br>Question:<br>Which rule shifts f left 2 and up 7? {
= g(x)=f(x+2)+7#Correct. Inside +2 shifts left and outside +7 shifts up.
~ g(x)=f(x-2)+7#This shifts right.
~ g(x)=f(x+7)+2#This switches values.
~ g(x)=f(x+2)-7#This shifts down.
}

::ALG2_U05_L04_Q18::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L04_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.4; MLA.A2.FN.5; MLA.A2.FN.6<br><br>Question:<br>Which rule shifts f right 4 and down 3? {
~ g(x)=f(x+4)-3#This shifts left.
= g(x)=f(x-4)-3#Correct. Inside -4 shifts right and outside -3 shifts down.
~ g(x)=f(x-3)-4#This switches values.
~ g(x)=f(x-4)+3#This shifts up.
}

::ALG2_U05_L04_Q19::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L04_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.4; MLA.A2.FN.5; MLA.A2.FN.6<br><br>Question:<br>Which transformation changes outputs? {
~ g(x)=f(x-5)#This changes inputs.
~ g(x)=f(x+5)#This changes inputs.
= g(x)=f(x)+5#Correct. Adding outside changes y-values.
~ g(x)=f(-x)#This changes inputs.
}

::ALG2_U05_L04_Q20::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L04_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.4; MLA.A2.FN.5; MLA.A2.FN.6<br><br>Question:<br>Which transformation changes inputs? {
~ g(x)=f(x)+5#This changes outputs.
~ g(x)=2f(x)#This changes outputs.
~ g(x)=-f(x)#This changes outputs.
= g(x)=f(x-5)#Correct. The change is inside the function argument.
}

::ALG2_U05_L04_Q21::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L04_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.4; MLA.A2.FN.5; MLA.A2.FN.6<br><br>Question:<br>What is the horizontal shift in g(x)=f(x+8)? {
= Left 8#Correct. Inside +8 shifts left.
~ Right 8#Inside direction is opposite.
~ Up 8#This is not outside.
~ Down 8#This is not outside.
}

::ALG2_U05_L04_Q22::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L04_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.4; MLA.A2.FN.5; MLA.A2.FN.6<br><br>Question:<br>What is the vertical shift in g(x)=f(x-2)-6? {
~ Up 6#The sign is negative.
= Down 6#Correct. The outside -6 shifts the graph down.
~ Right 2#That is the horizontal shift, not vertical.
~ Left 2#The horizontal direction is right.
}

::ALG2_U05_L04_Q23::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L04_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.4; MLA.A2.FN.5; MLA.A2.FN.6<br><br>Question:<br>What happens in g(x)=-2f(x)? {
~ Reflect across y-axis and stretch by 2#A negative input is needed for y-axis reflection.
~ Shift down 2#Multiplication is not a shift.
= Reflect across x-axis and vertically stretch by 2#Correct. The negative reflects outputs and factor 2 stretches.
~ Shift right 2#No inside shift appears.
}

::ALG2_U05_L04_Q24::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L04_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.4; MLA.A2.FN.5; MLA.A2.FN.6<br><br>Question:<br>Which rule is a vertical compression? {
~ g(x)=4f(x)#This is a vertical stretch.
~ g(x)=f(x)+0.25#This is a shift up.
~ g(x)=f(x-0.25)#This is a horizontal shift.
= g(x)=0.25f(x)#Correct. Outputs are multiplied by a factor between 0 and 1.
}

::ALG2_U05_L04_Q25::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L04_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.4; MLA.A2.FN.5; MLA.A2.FN.6<br><br>Question:<br>Why is f(x-3) a shift right? {
= Inputs must be 3 larger to produce the original output.#Correct. Inside transformations act opposite the sign shown.
~ The minus sign always means left.#Inside signs reverse direction.
~ It changes output values directly.#Inside changes affect inputs.
~ It reflects the graph.#A negative multiplier would indicate reflection.
}

